# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing here is a credential, and `src/env.ts` refuses to
# start on a known placeholder rather than booting with one — a placeholder that boots is a
# placeholder that reaches production.
#
# NAMING. `secret-hygiene` rejects a line whose NAME matches *SECRET*|*TOKEN*|*KEY* and whose value
# does not look like a placeholder. `micro-devplatform` hit that with a duration called
# `…_SECRET_OVERLAP_MINUTES` and renamed the variable rather than weakening the guard. So in this
# file the only names carrying credential vocabulary are credentials; every duration and count is
# `…_MINUTES`, `…_DAYS`, `…_MS` or `…_BATCH`. `env.test.ts` asserts that in both directions.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
#
# It holds the estate's AUDIT OF RECORD — the hash-chained mirror SD-15 says is the only thing that
# can answer "who did what, to whose data, and was it allowed" months later under dispute. Back it
# up off-host and restore-test it (SD-17): a truncate here leaves no gap for the chain to detect.
ADMIN_API_DATABASE_URL=postgres://admin_api:admin_api@127.0.0.1:5432/admin_api

# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Every route on this surface requires a verified operator token and
# there is no fallback credential, so identity is a HARD readiness dependency here — see the probe
# block in src/index.ts for why that differs from every other upstream below.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE SECRET THAT MAKES THE AUDIT MIRROR UNFORGEABLE.
#
# `POST /v1/events` is the intake for every other service's audit rows (17 §2, SD-15). The body is
# verified against this key over the EXACT bytes received, with a timing-safe comparison, BEFORE
# `JSON.parse` is called on it. Unsigned, that route is a forgery endpoint: anyone who could reach
# the port could write a row into the record a dispute is settled against, naming any operator for
# any action.
#
# The same key signs what this service emits, so every subscriber can verify it.
#
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 48
#
# NOT "at least 24 characters", WHICH IS WHAT THIS LINE USED TO SAY. A character floor is what let
# micro-org #142's 40-character `estate-only-outbox-secret-...` through every guard in the estate.
# `@cloudsforge/secrets` asks for the SHAPE of a generated key instead: the base64 or hex alphabet
# (a hyphen or an underscore is refused, because that is what a person typing a memorable value
# reaches for), 32 decoded BYTES, and a measured entropy floor. Leaving CHANGE_ME here is refused at
# boot, deliberately, and so is anything else somebody types by hand.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME

# The upstreams. All four are REQUIRED and none is optional: a missing URL would render as a
# permanently unavailable tile, and an operator reading a console during an incident cannot tell a
# tile that is down from a tile that was never configured. See the header of src/env.ts.
IDENTITY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001
LEDGER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4007
MARKET_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4013
BILLING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4009
# Forge Worlds' game service, for the Worlds screen. `nda` binds 4110.
NDA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4110

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THIS SERVICE'S OWN SCOPED SERVICE CREDENTIAL. Not shared: SD-05, SD-12.
#
# It is used in exactly ONE place — calls to the ledger — because `authorise` there refuses a user
# principal outright (ledger/src/server.ts). Market and billing get the OPERATOR'S OWN BEARER
# instead, forwarded verbatim, so their audit records which administrator acted. SD-11 calls that
# the one genuinely good decision in the nimbus admin proxies this service replaces.
#
# It needs `ledger:post` (to reverse an entry) and `ledger:read` (for the trial-balance tile).
# It needs nothing else. A token here holding more than that is a token whose blast radius exceeds
# what this service can do with it.
#
# ── DO NOT GENERATE THIS ONE. IT IS MINTED, NOT GENERATED ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# This line used to say `openssl rand -base64 32`, and that instruction cannot produce a working
# value: identity issues a service credential as `cfsc_` + base64url, against a revocable row, and
# `deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh` is what asks for one. `openssl` produces a key nothing has
# ever heard of, which authenticates to nothing.
#
# It is also NOT a JWT, whatever the variable's name suggests. Measured on the live estate on
# 2026-08-05 this variable held a 701-byte token that had EXPIRED 26 HOURS EARLIER on a container
# reporting healthy — micro-org #222. A token is read here once, at boot, so it is dead on the next
# restart at the latest; a credential confers nothing by itself and survives a restart. `env.ts`
# refuses a JWT here by name, and that refusal is the fix rather than an obstacle to it.
#
# ── AND IT IS EXCHANGED, NOT PRESENTED. THIS IS THE VARIABLE TO SET — micro-org #222 ───────────
#
# The credential is long-lived and worth nothing on its own. At the moment a peer is called,
# `ServiceTokenProvider` posts it to identity's `POST /service-tokens/exchange` and gets back an
# ordinary 600-second token, then re-mints before expiry. The exchange consumes nothing, so N
# replicas boot from this one value and a restart six days later still works.
#
# The 600 seconds is deliberately unchanged. Rotation IS expiry, and a longer-lived token only
# moves the cliff: `settlement` read a 600-second token once at boot, authenticated for ten minutes
# after each restart and was dead thereafter, producing 1,315 undelivered withdrawal attempts.
#
# Mint one: deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
ADMIN_API_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=CHANGE_ME

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ADMIN_API_SERVICE_TOKEN — A MIGRATION AID WITH A STATED END, NOT A MODE. DELETE IT.
#
# It exists only because `deploy/compose/docker-compose.estate.yml` still sets it and does not yet
# pass `ADMIN_API_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL`, which `estate-bootstrap.sh` has minted into `tokens.env` all
# along. It is held to exactly the same assertion as the credential above, so the expired JWT the
# estate is running today will NOT boot — which is the point.
#
# Set the credential, restart, then remove this line. The credential wins whenever both are set
# (`upstreams.ts`), so no window exists in which the dead token is presented.
#
# ADMIN_API_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4014
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
ADMIN_API_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by and in the expiry job's audit payload. Defaults to the
# hostname, which is the container id under compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
#
# It is NOT part of the audit actor: `audit_events_actor_is_a_principal` refuses
# `service:admin-api@replica`, because an actor is an identity and two replicas are one identity.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- the approval queue ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# How long a pending request may sit unanswered before the expiry job closes it. Default four
# hours: long enough that a second operator in another timezone can answer, short enough that a
# request raised during one incident is not approved during the next by somebody who was not in
# the room for either. An approval is consent to an action in a context, and the context does not
# keep.
# ADMIN_API_APPROVAL_TTL_MINUTES=240

# --- the audit chain verifier ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# How many rows one verification pass re-hashes. The pass resumes from the last checkpoint, so
# this is a ceiling on one run rather than on the total. SD-16 runs continuity verification
# nightly and calls a break a P0; the job's schedule is in src/jobs.ts (RECURRING).
# ADMIN_API_AUDIT_VERIFY_BATCH=5000

# --- retention ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Idempotency claims. It must outlive every caller's retry horizon: expiring a key EARLY means the
# next replay of it raises a SECOND approval request, and two approvals have two ids, so the
# ledger's own idempotency would not catch the second reversal. A claim that produced an artefact
# is kept regardless of age.
#
# Audit rows are NOT retained on a timer and there is no variable for it. They are the record a
# dispute is settled against and 13 §187 marks them "transactional, never dropped, never sampled".
# ADMIN_API_IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_DAYS=14

# --- WHICH ESTATE THIS IS ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# REQUIRED, with NO DEFAULT, and the absence of a default is the whole control. Every other
# required variable here fails loudly the first time it is used; this one would fail SILENTLY and
# LATE. An estate that guessed its own environment would stamp every backup it takes with the wrong
# label, and the artefacts would sit on disk looking correct until somebody restored one into the
# wrong estate and overwrote real balances.
#
# On 2026-08-05 the estate seeder ran `docker compose` against the MAINNET project regardless of
# the target it was given, TWICE. That is the defect this variable exists to make impossible.
#
# At boot it is compared against `estate_identity`, one row claimed once and immutable thereafter.
# A disagreement is a REFUSAL TO START, so a compose file pointed at the wrong estate is a
# container that will not boot rather than a wrong restore six weeks later.
#
# One of: mainnet | testnet | development
ADMIN_API_ESTATE_ENVIRONMENT=development

# Which compose project's volumes a backup taken here names — `cloudsforge-estate` (mainnet) or
# `cf-testnet`. DEFAULTED, unlike the environment above, and the asymmetry is deliberate: getting
# this wrong names the wrong volumes in a manifest, getting the environment wrong destroys money.
# The default matches compose's own (deploy/compose/mainnet.env omits CF_PROJECT for that reason).
# ADMIN_API_COMPOSE_PROJECT=cloudsforge-estate

# --- upstreams ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Absolute wall-clock ceiling per upstream call, across retries. Nimbus's admin proxies call bare
# `fetch` with no total-request timeout (routes/vault.ts:61, routes/pay.ts:73), so a hung
# ForgeKeyvault pins the identity service indefinitely. That hole is what this closes.
# ADMIN_API_UPSTREAM_DEADLINE_MS=5000

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# ADMIN_API_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains
# "test" — the database-backed tests truncate. CI exports it; a different spelling would silently
# skip them, and the workflow fails the build if it detects that.

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. Set the first
# in the estate, where one pod answers for mainnet and testnet and the gateway
# stamps `CF-Network` on every request. See micro-deploy
# `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
ADMIN_API_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=
